\DOC LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-code goal across one inlined
modular-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state with a
later one and recording the mathematical result of the squaring as a new
assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} giving an offset/count used in locating the call
site, namely how many extra setup instructions before the squaring body are
consumed. The second is a {string list} naming the result and input variables
of the squaring step: the first name becomes the output value stored in memory,
the second identifies the operand read. The goal retains its {eventually}
shape, with only the trailing state advanced:
{
   A ?- eventually ... s_i
   ================================================  LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC n ["u"; "t"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s_j
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained from {A} as follows. All assumptions mentioning
the old state — program-counter and stack-pointer readings, {bytes_loaded},
register and memory-content equations, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation
from the initial state — are discharged and re-added for the new state, with
the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the components the step may have modified,
and new assumptions are added for the pointer/return-address values set up by
the call; an initial trivial {MAYCHANGE} assumption from the start state is
dropped. Two further assumptions about the new state are introduced: a
memory-content equation saying that a particular buffer holds the named result
value, and an implication {t < p_521 ==> u = t EXP 2 MOD p_521} relating the
named result to the square of the named operand modulo the prime. Arithmetic
implications accumulated for earlier steps are retained, sometimes reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this {eventually} shape carrying a full set of machine-state
assumptions.

\ENDDOC
